bridge	A1	unconceived	imagining them as existing with no one at all conceiving them, which is the content of the thought	their being unconceived by you while you conceive them, which is a fact about your act
# P4's repugnancy holds only in the second sense: you would be conceiving them and not conceiving them at once.
# P3 needs the first sense: to think objects may exist without the mind is to represent them as unconceived.
# A1 moves from the impossibility of the act reading to the denial of the content reading.
# I am unsure here. Berkeley plainly treats the two as one claim, so this is the standard critics' reading rather than one the text marks.
# The objector's "imagine with nobody by to perceive" (S1) also shades into Berkeley's "omitting to frame the idea of a perceiver" (S2). But S2 concedes the same claim and then says what it amounts to, so I kept it one.
